WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.667 --> 00:00:15.634
Many music lovers and scholars consider Ludwig van Beethoven one of the greatest composers who ever lived.

2
00:00:15.635 --> 00:00:22.399
It's especially amazing that Beethoven composed many of his musical masterpieces after losing his hearing!

3
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.605
Ludwig van Beethoven was born in Bonn, Germany, in 1770.

4
00:00:27.606 --> 00:00:33.874
Both his father and grandfather were court musicians—musicians hired to play for royalty.

5
00:00:33.875 --> 00:00:40.145
Beethoven’s father was determined to teach his young son to play music, and to turn him into a prodigy.

6
00:00:40.146 --> 00:00:49.196
According to many stories, Beethoven was often crying as he practiced, and his father made him work very hard, often until late at night.

7
00:00:49.197 --> 00:00:53.790
When Beethoven was almost 12, he published his first composition.

8
00:00:53.791 --> 00:01:00.490
He had also been appointed as an assistant organist at the court—an impressive job for such a young musician.

9
00:01:00.491 --> 00:01:06.408
It was around this time that Beethoven left school and devoted himself entirely to music.

10
00:01:06.409 --> 00:01:10.502
As a result, he was never a very good speller.

11
00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:16.102
His letters show that he often made simple grammatical errors, even later in his life.

12
00:01:16.103 --> 00:01:21.943
As Beethoven began earning money from his music, his family grew more dependent upon his income.

13
00:01:21.944 --> 00:01:25.890
The Beethovens had once been wealthy, but the father was an alcoholic.

14
00:01:25.891 --> 00:01:31.216
His serious drinking problem had led the family into financial decline.

15
00:01:31.217 --> 00:01:38.740
When Beethoven turned 18 years old, the court decided to send him to Vienna, which was the center of the music world at that time.

16
00:01:38.741 --> 00:01:44.969
Beethoven wanted to stay in Vienna and study with the great composer Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart.

17
00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:53.025
But back in Bonn, disaster struck. His mother fell ill and died, and Beethoven had to return home to Bonn.

18
00:01:53.026 --> 00:01:57.698
The next years were probably a very stressful time in the Beethoven household.

19
00:01:57.699 --> 00:02:04.545
Beethoven’s father was still drinking, and Beethoven had two younger brothers who needed his financial support.

20
00:02:04.546 --> 00:02:11.641
Beethoven's life was very busy as he tried to take care of his family and practice and develop his music.

21
00:02:11.642 --> 00:02:17.449
Yet it was also a good time for his career as he took on more responsibilities at court.

22
00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:22.524
He also expanded his social circle with other musicians and some prosperous friends.

23
00:02:22.525 --> 00:02:29.655
In 1790 an important European political figure—the emperor of the Holy Roman Empire, Joseph II—died,

24
00:02:29.656 --> 00:02:34.039
and Beethoven was invited to compose the funeral ode.

25
00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:37.262
This was a huge honor for a young composer.

26
00:02:37.263 --> 00:02:40.830
While Beethoven's composition was never played at the emperor's funeral,

27
00:02:40.831 --> 00:02:46.104
it did eventually attract the attention of another well-known composer—Joseph Haydn.

28
00:02:46.105 --> 00:02:53.159
By 1792 Beethoven was traveling back to Vienna to study with Haydn as well as other composers.

29
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.080
During this time Beethoven continued to compose music.

30
00:02:57.081 --> 00:03:02.083
His compositions were very different from anything anyone had heard before.

31
00:03:02.084 --> 00:03:07.214
Beethoven included new sounds and paired up instruments in new and creative ways.

32
00:03:07.215 --> 00:03:17.990
He also became known in Vienna for his playing of the piano; he received many invitations to play at the homes of noble families and aristocrats.

33
00:03:17.991 --> 00:03:25.306
By the time Beethoven was around 28 years old, disaster had struck again—he realized he was losing his hearing.

34
00:03:25.307 --> 00:03:32.552
Over the years he became completely deaf, sometimes isolating himself from others, and growing bitter about his condition.

35
00:03:32.553 --> 00:03:39.130
Even though he could no longer hear his compositions being played, Beethoven kept writing music.

36
00:03:39.131 --> 00:03:45.619
He often wandered country roads in villages near Vienna, and in sketchbooks, he wrote down his ideas.

37
00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:50.322
His music grew more sensitive and powerful because of his personal struggles.

38
00:03:50.323 --> 00:03:55.772
Beethoven never had luck finding love, and this added to his loneliness and dark spirit.

39
00:03:55.773 --> 00:04:01.453
His piece Moonlight Sonata is regarded as one of his most beautiful works.

40
00:04:01.454 --> 00:04:06.968
It is believed that it was inspired by the tragic love he felt for a girl he couldn't marry.

41
00:04:06.969 --> 00:04:10.882
Beethoven's nine symphonies are also considered masterpieces.

42
00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:14.715
A symphony is a long musical work played by an orchestra.

43
00:04:14.716 --> 00:04:20.473
Beethoven wrote complicated compositions that evoke many different moods and feelings.

44
00:04:20.474 --> 00:04:24.738
Each symphony has a different theme and expresses a different emotion.

45
00:04:24.739 --> 00:04:30.697
His Symphony No. 3 was written in honor of Napoleon, whom Beethoven admired for a time.

46
00:04:30.698 --> 00:04:34.787
Symphony No. 6 was inspired by the beauty found in nature.

47
00:04:34.788 --> 00:04:42.146
In one section Beethoven has woodwind instruments imitate the sounds of nightingales, cuckoos, and quails.

48
00:04:42.147 --> 00:04:47.361
His most popular symphony is probably his final one—Symphony No. 9.

49
00:04:47.362 --> 00:04:54.416
It is a very powerful piece of music, one in which Beethoven uses both an orchestra and a chorus of singers.

50
00:04:54.417 --> 00:05:01.038
When it was first played, the audience loved it so much, they clapped for a very long time after the concert ended.

51
00:05:01.039 --> 00:05:06.944
Because he was deaf, Beethoven was completely unaware of the thunderous applause.

52
00:05:06.945 --> 00:05:13.248
Finally someone turned him in the direction of the audience so he could see their enthusiastic clapping and cheering.

53
00:05:13.249 --> 00:05:20.402
Ludwig van Beethoven died in Vienna on March 26, 1827 at age 57.

54
00:05:20.403 --> 00:05:26.716
He left an incredible musical legacy that includes some of the most important musical works of all time.

55
00:05:26.717 --> 00:05:37.850
It is hard to believe that he created many of these great works without being able to hear a single note.
